If you want to make robots with AI using real-world human data, then access to lots of humans, especially if affordable, helps.

GENE-26.5 is @gs_ai_'s robotics-native multimodal foundation model, trained on over 200,000 hours of real human hand data (motion, force, touch) and running on a 54-DoF bimanual system. Scaling that human data four times lifted real-robot success rates from 16.6% to 65.6% on long-horizon dexterous tasks, using the same model weights with zero fine-tuning for this exact sequence.

@IlirAliu_: "Are you kidding me??? It grasps multiple objects in different ways, all at once with a single hand??? No pauses. 1x speed. You know those tiny coordinated movements you do without thinking? Robots couldn’t reliably do that before. Now they can. Today. This is the video you’ll send to my friends, outside of our bubble, when they say 'robots are still just demos.'"

